me, and its glow completely. Retain exactly one small bench fire as the sole flame, and arrange one loupe and one pair of tongs in isolated clear shapes beside it so that bench, loupe, tongs, and single small fire survive thumbnail and grayscale reduction without the surrounding tool clutter becoming a second primary marker. Remove every portable bedroom tabletop object other than the open velvet casket and reconstruct each vacated table or console as a bare white-and-gold surface. Retain one small white-and-gold table beside the bed as the sole object-bearing surface, with the open casket prominent on it and its two rows of six large slots—ten occupied and two unmistakably empty—remaining legible at thumbnail and in grayscale. Rebuild the recess, bench fronts, and every under-bench plane in pale white, cream, or very light umber; remove the filled dark shadow shapes entirely and carry depth only with thin contour line and overlapping pale planes. The single small fire may remain the sole concentrated dark-warm accent. Replace it with exactly one compact circular lens in a thin metal housing, shown in clear side-three-quarter view with its lens opening visible and scaled recognizably against the adjacent tongs; give it no brim, tall cylindrical crown, or vessel-like cavity. Keep the loupe and the single pair of tongs isolated from the secondary bench tools.
